%[text] # A Hierarchical Bayesian VAR in a Few Commands
%[text] This example builds the hierarchical Bayesian VAR of Giannone, Lenza and Primiceri (2012) on U.S. macroeconomic data, but its purpose is not to reproduce the paper - it is to show how the **SVAR toolbox** and the **Econometrics Toolbox** fit together into a short, readable workflow. Each section highlights one or two commands.
%[text] The idea is hierarchical. A conjugate Minnesota prior on the VAR coefficients depends on a handful of *hyperparameters* $ \\gamma $ - overall tightness, two dummy-observation tightnesses, and a residual-variance scale. Rather than fix them by hand, we treat them as unknown and infer them from the data through the marginal likelihood:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ p(\\gamma \\mid Y) \\propto p(Y \\mid \\gamma), p(\\gamma) $
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Contents"}
%[text] Commands showcased along the way:
%[text] - `hyperprior` - a prior on a hyperparameter, as an object
%[text] - `estimateResidualVariances` - the inverse-Wishart scale from the data
%[text] - `glp` - tune the hyperparameters, and sample them
%[text] - `estimate` and `simulate` - the conjugate posterior, from the Econometrics Toolbox
%[text] - `svar.varmFromCoefficients` and `svar.irf` - structural impulse responses \
numLags          = 5;
forecastHorizons = 1:8;
irfHorizon       = 20;
policyShock      = 7;      % federal funds rate, ordered last
%%
%[text] ## The data
%[text] The sample is the Stock and Watson (2008) dataset used in the paper: seven quarterly series from 1959 to 2008, held in a `timetable`. The six real and nominal series enter in annualised log-levels; the federal funds rate is a rate in levels. A `stackedplot` is the quickest way to eyeball a multivariate timetable.
load("data.mat", "tt");
Y           = tt{:,:};
seriesNames = string(tt.Properties.VariableNames);
numSeries   = width(tt);
stackedplot(tt) %[output:7bb0f0ee]
title("Stock-Watson (2008) series") %[output:7bb0f0ee]
%%
%[text] ## Priors as objects
%[text] A `hyperprior` packages one distribution on one hyperparameter, together with the bounds and starting point an optimiser needs. Following the paper we put Gamma priors with modes 0.2, 1 and 1 on the overall tightness $ \\lambda $, the sum-of-coefficients tightness $ \\mu $, and the dummy-initial-observation tightness $ \\delta $.
lambda1Prior = hyperprior("Gamma", 0.2, 0.4, Bounds = [1e-4, 5]);
lambda4Prior = hyperprior("Gamma", 1, 1, Bounds = [1e-4, 50]);
lambda5Prior = hyperprior("Gamma", 1, 1, Bounds = [1e-4, 50]);
%[text] The object carries a stable log density, so plotting the prior is a one-liner. This is the reference prior on the overall Minnesota tightness.
lambdaGrid = linspace(0, 2, 400);
plot(lambdaGrid, exp(lambda1Prior.logpdf(lambdaGrid)), LineWidth = 1.5) %[output:3bfcd12a]
title("Hyperprior on the overall tightness \lambda") %[output:3bfcd12a]
xlabel("\lambda"); ylabel("density"); grid on %[output:3bfcd12a]
%%
%[text] ## The residual-variance scale
%[text] The prior on the innovation covariance is centred on the residual variances of univariate autoregressions - one per series. `estimateResidualVariances` computes them directly from the data.
psi0 = svar.estimateResidualVariances(Y, 1, Method = "conditional");
disp(table(seriesNames', psi0', VariableNames = ["Series", "AR residual variance"])) %[output:5b325794]
%[text] The paper's own prior on this scale is an inverse-Gamma with shape and scale both equal to $ 0.02^2 $ - a density so diffuse it has neither a mean nor a variance. That cannot be described by a mode and a standard deviation, so `hyperprior` takes it in its native parameterisation instead, one prior per series.
psiPrior = arrayfun(@(v) hyperprior("InverseGamma", 0.02^2, 0.02^2, ...
    Parameterization = "native", X0 = v, Bounds = [v/100, v*100]), psi0);
%%
%[text] ## Tuning the hyperparameters
%[text] `glp` maximises the log marginal likelihood plus the hyperpriors, then returns the Minnesota prior built at that maximiser. Passing a `hyperprior` for a field makes it free; passing a scalar fixes it. Here the lag-decay `lambda3` is fixed at 1 while the three tightnesses and the seven residual variances are tuned. The finite-difference step is kept coarse: the marginal likelihood is mildly noisy, and fmincon's default step is too small to see past that noise.
quietOptim = optimoptions("fmincon", ...
    Display                  = "off", ...
    FiniteDifferenceStepSize = 1e-4, ...
    FunctionTolerance        = 1e-12, ...
    StepTolerance            = 1e-12, ...
    ConstraintTolerance      = 1e-12, ...
    MaxIterations            = 1000, ...
    MaxFunctionEvaluations   = 20000);
priorMdl = glp(numSeries, numLags, tt, ...
    lambda1      = lambda1Prior, ...
    lambda3      = 1, ...
    lambda4      = lambda4Prior, ...
    lambda5      = lambda5Prior, ...
    Vc           = 10e6, ...
    Psi          = psiPrior, ...
    OptimOptions = quietOptim);
%[text] The result is a fully specified conjugate Minnesota prior - a `svar.minnesotamniwbvarm` object with the sum-of-coefficients and dummy-initial-observation pseudo-observations already folded in.
priorMdl %[output:563dd7ce]
%[text] Because the prior is conjugate, its marginal likelihood is available in closed form through `logMarginalLikelihood` - the very quantity `glp` just maximised.
logMarginalLikelihood(priorMdl) %[output:0b63f081]
%%
%[text] ## Integrating over the hyperparameters
%[text] The mode is only half the story. The contribution of the paper is to account for uncertainty *about* the hyperparameters, by sampling their posterior with a Metropolis step. The same `glp` call does this when asked for draws: it locates the mode, builds a proposal from the curvature there, and explores around it. Conditional on each hyperparameter draw, the VAR coefficients and covariance come from their exact Normal-inverse-Wishart posterior.
rng(0, "twister")
[priorMdl, info, chain] = glp(numSeries, numLags, tt, ...
    lambda1       = lambda1Prior, ...
    lambda3       = 1, ...
    lambda4       = lambda4Prior, ...
    lambda5       = lambda5Prior, ...
    Vc            = 10e6, ...
    Psi           = psiPrior, ...
    OptimOptions  = quietOptim, ...
    NumDraws      = 2000, ...
    BurnIn        = 2000, ...
    ProposalScale = 0.8);
disp("Acceptance rate: " + chain.AcceptanceRate) %[output:4456c957]
%[text] The chain returns draws of every hyperparameter alongside the VAR parameters they imply. The posterior of the overall tightness sits well inside the reference prior - the data are informative about how much to shrink.
histogram(chain.lambda1, 40, Normalization = "pdf", EdgeColor = "none") %[output:7e413f9c]
hold on %[output:7e413f9c]
plot(lambdaGrid, exp(lambda1Prior.logpdf(lambdaGrid)), LineWidth = 1.5) %[output:7e413f9c]
hold off %[output:7e413f9c]
title("Posterior and prior of \lambda"); xlabel("\lambda"); ylabel("density") %[output:7e413f9c]
legend("posterior", "prior"); xlim([0 2]) %[output:7e413f9c]
%%
%[text] ## The posterior at the mode
%[text] For a single best-guess VAR, `estimate` returns the analytic conjugate posterior of the coefficients and covariance - a standard Econometrics Toolbox call, here served by the Minnesota prior object.
posterior        = estimate(priorMdl, Display = "off");
modeCoefficients = reshape(posterior.Mu, [], numSeries);
modeSigma        = posterior.Omega/(posterior.DoF + numSeries + 1);
modeVAR          = svar.varmFromCoefficients(posterior, modeCoefficients, modeSigma);
disp(modeVAR) %[output:27bde75e]
%%
%[text] ## From hyperparameter draws to VAR paths
%[text] Each draw in the chain is one VAR. `svar.varmFromCoefficients` turns a draw of coefficients and covariance into a `varm` model, which the Econometrics Toolbox then `simulate`s forward for a predictive path, and which `svar.irf` maps into structural impulse responses. The monetary-policy shock is identified by a Cholesky factor with the funds rate ordered last.
numDraws      = size(chain.Sigma, 3);
presample     = Y(end-numLags+1:end, :);
forecastDraws = zeros(numel(forecastHorizons), numSeries, numDraws);
irfDraws      = zeros(irfHorizon, numSeries, numDraws);
for draw = 1:numDraws
    sigma   = chain.Sigma(:,:,draw);
    impact  = chol(sigma, "lower");
    drawVAR = svar.varmFromCoefficients(posterior, chain.Coefficients(:,:,draw), sigma);
    forecastDraws(:,:,draw) = simulate(drawVAR, max(forecastHorizons), Y0 = presample);
    irfDraws(:,:,draw)      = svar.irf(drawVAR, impact(:, policyShock), irfHorizon - 1);
end
disp("Simulated " + numDraws + " forecast paths and impulse-response draws.") %[output:491f6a2e]
%%
%[text] ## Predictive density for GDP growth
%[text] Collapsing the forecast paths for real GDP into annualised average growth gives a predictive density - the distribution of where GDP is headed, with hyperparameter uncertainty already integrated out. The one-year-ahead density is shown with its median marked.
gdpGrowth = averageAnnualGrowth(squeeze(forecastDraws(:,1,:)), Y(end,1), forecastHorizons);
oneYear   = gdpGrowth(4, :);
histogram(oneYear, 40, EdgeColor = "none") %[output:15fd0d91]
xline(median(oneYear), LineWidth = 1.5) %[output:15fd0d91]
title("One-year-ahead average GDP growth") %[output:15fd0d91]
xlabel("annualised average growth, percent"); ylabel("draws"); xlim([-15 10]) %[output:15fd0d91]
%[text] The point and interval forecasts at the reported horizons:
reported = [1 4 8];
disp(table(reported', median(gdpGrowth(reported,:), 2), std(gdpGrowth(reported,:), 0, 2), ... %[output:group:8cf73b96] %[output:8b41c1db]
    VariableNames = ["Quarters ahead", "Median growth", "Std"])) %[output:group:8cf73b96] %[output:8b41c1db]
%%
%[text] ## Impulse responses to a monetary policy shock
%[text] Finally, the structural impulse responses. The solid line is the response at the posterior mode; the shaded band is the 16th-to-84th percentile across draws, so it reflects uncertainty about the hyperparameters as well as the coefficients. The responses match the usual narrative of a monetary tightening: output and prices fall, the funds rate jumps on impact and reverts.
impact  = chol(modeSigma, "lower");
modeIRF = scaleToPercent(svar.irf(modeVAR, impact(:, policyShock), irfHorizon - 1));
bandIRF = scaleToPercent(quantile(irfDraws, [0.16 0.84], 3));
horizons = 0:(irfHorizon - 1);
t = tiledlayout(2, 4, TileSpacing = "compact", Padding = "compact"); %[output:895a10a0]
for series = 1:numSeries
    nexttile %[output:895a10a0]
    fill([horizons, fliplr(horizons)], ...
        [bandIRF(:,series,1); flipud(bandIRF(:,series,2))]', ...
        [0.8 0.85 0.95], EdgeColor = "none")
    hold on
    plot(horizons, modeIRF(:, series), "k", LineWidth = 1.2)
    yline(0, ":")
    hold off
    title(seriesNames(series)); xlim([0 irfHorizon-1])
end
title(t, "Responses to a monetary policy shock") %[output:895a10a0]
%%
%[text] ## Summary
%[text] Six toolbox commands carried the whole workflow: `hyperprior` and `estimateResidualVariances` to state the prior, `glp` to both tune and sample the hyperparameters, and `estimate`, `simulate`, `svar.varmFromCoefficients` and `svar.irf` to turn the result into forecasts and structural impulse responses. The heavy lifting - the analytic marginal likelihood, the conjugate posterior, the Metropolis step - lives inside those calls, so the script stays close to the economics.
%[text] The numbers reproduce the paper's medium-scale results closely; what changes here is the emphasis, from replication to the commands that make it a short exercise.
function scaled = scaleToPercent(irf)
%scaleToPercent Put every response in percent.
%   Series 1-6 enter as four times a log level, so a unit response is a
%   quarter of a log point; the funds rate is already a rate.
scaled          = irf*100;
scaled(:,1:6,:) = scaled(:,1:6,:)/4;
end
function growth = averageAnnualGrowth(levelForecast, lastLevel, horizons)
%averageAnnualGrowth Annualised average growth over h quarters, in percent.
growth = (levelForecast - lastLevel).*(100./horizons(:));
end

